<section class="section section--cream"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="section-head reveal"><div><p class="eyebrow">What we believe</p><h2>Four principles behind <em>every garden</em></h2></div></div>
  <div class="areas"><div class="area reveal" data-delay="0"><svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".18"/><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21zM6 26L19 13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><h3>Plants first</h3><p>We are planting designers at heart. Every layout is drawn to give the plants the best possible conditions, and every plant is chosen for its place.</p></div><div class="area reveal" data-delay="1"><svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".18"/><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21zM6 26L19 13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><h3>Right plant, right place</h3><p>We design for your soil, aspect and microclimate, so the garden thrives without constant intervention.</p></div><div class="area reveal" data-delay="2"><svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".18"/><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21zM6 26L19 13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><h3>Year-round interest</h3><p>A garden should earn its keep in February as well as June. Structure, bark, seedheads and evergreens carry it through winter.</p></div><div class="area reveal" data-delay="3"><svg viewBox="0 0 32 32" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".18"/><path d="M6 26C6 13 14 5 27 5c0 13-8 21-21 21zM6 26L19 13" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><h3>Gardens for living</h3><p>Beautiful, but practical: somewhere to eat, play, work and sit quietly, designed around the way you live.</p></div></div>
</div></section>
<section class="band">
  <div class="band__media" data-parallax="0.18"><img src="/assets/img/country-topiary.webp" srcset="/assets/img/country-topiary-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/country-topiary.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Clipped yew topiary on a broad lawn in an English country garden" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <div class="wrap reveal"><blockquote>&ldquo;Plant for February first. If the garden looks good then, it will look <em>wonderful</em> in June.&rdquo;</blockquote><cite>A Fraser &amp; Morris rule of thumb</cite></div>
</section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap split split--rev">
  <div class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Writing &amp; plants</p>
    <h2>Curious about <em>plants</em></h2>
    <p>Good planting design comes from looking: at gardens, at wild plant communities, at nurseries and at the way plants behave in different soils and seasons. We visit gardens and nurseries throughout the year and bring what we learn back into our planting plans.</p>
    <p>Dahlias, crab apples, alliums, grasses and woodland plants appear again and again in our work, because they are generous, long-lasting and beautiful as they fade.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="reveal-img" style="aspect-ratio:4/3;overflow:hidden"><img src="/assets/img/dahlias.webp" srcset="/assets/img/dahlias-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/dahlias.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Coral, peach and cream dahlias in full bloom" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover"></div>
</div></section>
